WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
-How are you, Neil?
-Hey. Hey. I'm doing great

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:04.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, sorry.
The universe is great.

00:00:04.304 --> 00:00:05.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's Earth that's messed up.
-Alright.

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:07.707 size:75% position:38%
Thank you for making
the clarification.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:10.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, we are coming up
on an election,

00:00:10.744 --> 00:00:13.046 size:81% position:41%
and it just feels like
anything might happen.

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:17.550
And now I'm hearing tell tha
there might be an asteroid

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
heading towards Earth
on Election Day.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:21.388 size:75% position:38% line:92%
True or false, Neil?

00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:23.323 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-It's true. It's true.

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:26.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's about -- It's a
refrigerator-sized asteroid.

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:28.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
And given the uncertainty
in this orbit,

00:00:28.461 --> 00:00:30.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
there's a small sliver of
that uncertainty

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:34.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
that includes colliding
with Earth on November 2nd

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:35.268 line:92%
-And what would be the fallout

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:38.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a refrigerator-sized
meteor hitting us?

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.072 line:92%
-Yeah, I mean, you would see i

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you were anywhere in
sight line of when it hit.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:44.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's going, you know,
10 or 20 miles per second.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:47.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you hit air,
even though air feels like

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:49.716
you can just move through it
if you're going really fast,

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:51.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's like you are hitting
a brick wall.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:53.753
So it would explode on impact.
You would see it all around.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:56.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
There would be
a tremendous meteor shower

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.758 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, no, it's not going to
harm anyone, no.

00:00:58.758 --> 00:00:59.826 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
But just the idea.

00:00:59.826 --> 00:01:02.095 size:84% position:42%
It's like, you know,
the universe is saying,

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.563 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
"Here, take that."

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.065 line:92%
-The first I've heard in month

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:07.767 line:92%
that's not going to harm anyone.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:08.268 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes.

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This must be
a very strange time to be

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
a scientist
and to see how people,

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:16.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly American people
are reacting to data.

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.779 size:75% position:38%
Is it as frustrating
as I imagine it is

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:23.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
as you watch
the response to COVID?

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:24.017 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
That's a "yes."

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.221
It looks like the clenched fists
are a classic "yes" from Neil.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:31.157 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, that's my miming
of how it feels.

00:01:31.157 --> 00:01:33.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I keep wondering
"What am I doing?"

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
And all these others
who are on this landscape

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:38.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
of science education --
they have YouTube channels

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:39.799 size:75% position:38% line:92%
and books and video.

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:42.435 line:92%
And then I have to tell myself

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:46.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Maybe, without all of this,
it would be much worse."

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:49.008
See, that's why -- Otherwise
nothing is working, right?

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:52.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I don't know how else
to feel good about any of this

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:54.647 line:92%
But, yeah, it's frustrating.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:58.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we went into that with the
whole flat-Earth people.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:01.020 line:92%
You know, I don't understand

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think science
needs to be taught

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:07.460
not as a satchel of facts,
but as a way of understandin

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:09.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
what is objectively true
in nature.

00:02:09.829 --> 00:02:12.198 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then, you know,
as evidence comes and goes

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:12.832 size:75% position:38% line:92%
"Okay, they're good.

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:13.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's good evidence.
Oh, no, no."

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:17.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you can sort of
ride the surf into the futur

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
of our understanding
of how the universe works.

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's
the only way to assure

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.943 align:start size:94% position:6%
that civilization will
survive ourselves.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:26.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, that speaks to the fact
that this is fluid.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:30.683
Obviously, we're learning more
about this pandemic every day,

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's very unfair
to ask people, on day one,

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:35.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
to know all the answers
we have on day 200.

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:40.994
You have described it as sort of
akin to an alien invasion.

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.329 line:92%
Can you explain that analogy

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, sure.
I mean, think about it.

00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:47.967 align:start size:94% position:6%
Suppose aliens came,
and they were just going to be

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
the enemies
of all humans, right?

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.504 line:92%
We'd band together, wouldn't we?

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.273
We would say,
"Let's put down our differences,

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
because we have a common enemy
that we must defeat."

00:02:56.976 --> 00:03:00.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, the coronavirus, COVID
is exactly that, right?

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't care
what your skin color is,

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:05.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
what country you're born in,
who you worship,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.452 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
who you sleep with.

00:03:06.452 --> 00:03:09.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't distinguish.
It doesn't care.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:12.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
And all the things that
tribally divide us,

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:14.561 line:92%
it only cares if you're human.

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:16.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so this is a test.
It's a test.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:21.367
How do we, as a species,
react to a common threat?

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.537 line:92%
And I rate us a C-minus in

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:27.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
how we have collectively
reacted to this.

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:30.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
To have one state open up
its restrictions

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:33.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
while another state
doesn't that's adjacent to it?

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:36.282 size:84% position:42%
And I think to myself,
that's like designating

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:38.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
a peeing section
of a swimming pool.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:43.523
That's exactly what that's like,
so I don't know what to say.

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:46.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I think you've said i
pretty well right there.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:50.897 align:start size:94% position:6%
You -- This season of "Cosmos"
is about possible worlds.

00:03:50.897 --> 00:03:52.932 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is sort of other worlds
that might exist,

00:03:52.932 --> 00:03:56.736
which I think probably sound
pretty attractive to people.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.505
Tell us a little bit about it.
-Yeah, yeah. Thanks for asking

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.542 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, possible worlds --
the obvious interpretation is,

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:07.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
there are exoplanets that we
might visit or populate

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.047 size:78% position:39% line:92%
just to escape Earth.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:11.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think,
more on a deeper level,

00:04:11.451 --> 00:04:16.789
a possible world is -- think o
the possible futures of Eart

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:17.924 line:92%
that we have control over.

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:21.294 align:start size:94% position:6%
Each of those different path
is a different world.

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:25.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
So "possible worlds"
is very broadly interprete

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:30.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
in terms of how we can
harness the power,

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:32.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
the awesome power,
of the methods and tools o

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:37.810
science and technology and
engineering to shape a futur

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
that -- where we will not only
survive, but thrive.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:45.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's a future of Earth
where our descendants

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:46.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
will be proud for what
we have done,

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
rather than embarrassed fo
what we didn't do

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:52.692
or for the wanton neglect
that we have expressed

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
on trying to sustain
our relationship

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the ecosystem
that has sustained us.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:05:02.302
-Well, thank you for alway
being a voice for science.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:05.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know, at times,
it sounds like or seems like

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:07.774
no one's listening, but I assure
you a lot of people are.

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:08.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's always a pleasure
to see you, Neil.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:10.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanks for making time for us.
-Well, thank you.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.145 line:92%
And "Cosmos" is all about hope

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:13.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, every episode,
we could say,

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:16.916 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You're all going to die,"
but we pose the problem

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:18.051 line:92%
and we give a thread of hope

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:20.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's what
we all need right now.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I am a little worried tha
next season

00:05:21.454 --> 00:05:25.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
is going to be "Cosmos: We'r
All Going to Die."

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:25.792 size:78% position:39% line:92%
But the third season,

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:27.727 align:start size:94% position:6%
the optimistic season,
of "Cosmos"

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.395 line:92%
premieres Tuesday on Fox.

